---
<Base lang={lang} path="/events/elixir/" title="Elixir Scramble: How to Win" description="A 30-minute alliance-versus-alliance point battlefield: five stages, control-based scoring, Healing Tent priority, teleport rules, and the wall mistake that ends your run in 10 minutes.">
  <GuidePage title="Elixir Scramble: How to Win" description="A 30-minute alliance-versus-alliance point battlefield: five stages, control-based scoring, Healing Tent priority, teleport rules, and the wall mistake that ends your run in 10 minutes." lang={lang}>
    <p>Elixir Scramble is a <strong>30-minute alliance-versus-alliance battlefield</strong> where the winner is decided by points, not by who kills more troops. Alliances that treat it like a deathmatch lose to alliances that treat it like a map-control game — every time.</p>
    <p>This guide covers the five stages of the event, where the points actually come from, teleport rules, defense logic, and the one mistake that ends a run before it starts.</p>

    <h2 id="event-structure">The Five Stages</h2>
    <p>Every scramble runs the same arc, about six minutes per stage across the half hour:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Matching</strong> — the system pairs alliances.</li>
      <li><strong>Entry prep</strong> — you load in, position, and get your squads off the wall (see <a href="#wall-warning">the wall warning</a> — this stage is where runs are lost).</li>
      <li><strong>Early strongholds</strong> — the first buildings unlock and the opening land-grab happens.</li>
      <li><strong>Mid decisive battles</strong> — the contested center becomes the point pivot; most of the event is won or lost here.</li>
      <li><strong>Late accumulation</strong> — held buildings tick out the final points; the alliance that stayed active to the last second collects the difference.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="registration">Registration and Timing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Players sign up as <strong>Team A or Team B</strong>, or the <strong>R5 signs the alliance up</strong> directly.</li>
      <li><strong>After registration closes, nobody can be added.</strong> No late joiners, no substitutes — if half your alliance sleeps through sign-up, you fight short-handed.</li>
      <li>Scheduling example: a scramble that starts at <strong>3PM CST runs at 9PM UK</strong>. Check the event timer in your own timezone and set an alarm — the event rewards being online at the exact start, not five minutes in.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="points">Where Points Come From</h2>
    <p>The core rule, and the one most alliances get wrong: <strong>points come from CONTROL, not from kills.</strong> Killing enemy squads earns nothing directly. Holding buildings earns points every tick. A squad that never fights but stands on a high-value building outperforms a squad that wipes three enemies and stands on nothing.</p>
    <p>The building hierarchy:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Healing Tents</strong> — the second highest point building for the first 10 minutes. They heal <strong>15 soldiers per 10 seconds</strong>, which keeps your fighting squads on the map instead of walking home. Note: <strong>speedups are useless inside the event</strong> — healing runs on the tent, not on your items.</li>
      <li><strong>Camps</strong> — spawn later in the event and become the point-gathering targets; grab them when they appear.</li>
      <li><strong>The central Elixir Castle</strong> — the highest-value point source on the map. This is the <strong>priority target for your strongest players</strong>: secure it and hold it. It is also the most contested, so expect the decisive battles to happen around it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="teleports">Teleport Rules</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Free teleport has a 2-minute cooldown</strong> — and teleporting <strong>cancels any marches you have out</strong>. Never reposition while your squads are mid-march to a target.</li>
      <li><strong>Direct Teleport is not allowed</strong> inside the event. You cannot buy your way around the map.</li>
      <li>The standard play: <strong>base your city at the castle</strong> and dispatch marches outward from there. One reposition, then a short walk for everything else — this minimizes the cooldown problem entirely.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="defense">Buildings and Defense Logic</h2>
    <p>Claimed buildings do <strong>not</strong> need a garrison to keep generating points. That leads to a simple rule:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Only defend a building if it is actually contested.</strong> Parking squads on everything you own is point-neutral and wastes your army.</li>
      <li>When a building does come under attack, <strong>multiple squads can defend it</strong> — stack the defense rather than feeding squads in one at a time.</li>
      <li>You can also <strong>choose not to defend at all</strong>: let a low-value building go, keep your squads alive, and retake it once the enemy marches elsewhere. Points tick back the moment you re-claim.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="wall-warning">CRITICAL: Remove Squads From Your Wall Before the Start</h2>
    <blockquote>If your squads are still on your wall when Elixir Scramble begins, you can lose your troops in under 10 minutes — and your event is over for the remaining 20.</blockquote>
    <p>Wall squads get caught in the opening wave of attacks, and dead squads inside the event are dead for the full half hour. During the entry-prep stage, the checklist is:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Pull every squad off the wall.</li>
      <li>Confirm your marches are empty and cooldowns are ready.</li>
      <li>Position at the castle.</li>
      <li>Then and only then, start dispatching.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="winning-strategy">The 30-Minute Game Plan</h2>
    <p>The winning pattern, condensed:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Be online at the start.</strong> The early land-grab of strongholds decides who plays from ahead.</li>
      <li><strong>Stay active the full 30 minutes.</strong> Points accumulate the entire time; the alliance that checks out at minute 20 donates the last third of the event.</li>
      <li><strong>Hold, don&apos;t overextend.</strong> Secure what you have before opening a third front. A building you hold to the end beats two buildings you briefly touched.</li>
      <li><strong>Rotate and reinforce.</strong> Squads on contested buildings need fresh bodies; rotate tired squads back through Healing Tents.</li>
      <li><strong>Prioritize high-point buildings.</strong> The Elixir Castle first, Healing Tents early, Camps when they spawn.</li>
      <li><strong>Never go solo.</strong> A single squad attacking a defended point dies alone and contributes nothing. Move with your alliance or don&apos;t move.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Elixir Scramble rewards discipline over power. A weaker alliance that holds the right buildings and never stops rotating will beat a stronger alliance chasing kills — because kills were never worth anything.</p>
